scope='Nine new public proofs and three definitions close two source subobligations: arbitrary feasible initialization (printed p.3 / PDF p.15) and a recursive mean/count summary (printed p.6 / PDF p.18). They are not nine additional numbered book results. Four existing Mean proofs and every prior FTL proof are retained; six validation proofs are not book results.'
model='Deterministic scalar squared-loss guessing. Fix initial c before observations. Lean time t counts observed labels y0,...,y(t-1); predict before observing yt, then update the local state with yt.'
domain='The algebraic identities hold for all real initial values and labels. Source feasibility requires c in [0,1] and only the strict-past labels yi in [0,1] for i<t; the current and future labels are unrestricted.'
boundary='Compiled local BODY-approved package. Combined project, reader FINAL, native acceptance and PR delivery are separate pending gates. No new regret rate for arbitrary c. The first-round quarter and the prior refined/logarithmic theorem remain specific to c=1/2.'
remaining='Chapter 1 remains open: W/V action-domain mapping and the logarithmic lower-bound/source-claim audit are required, together with actual contributor reconciliation. Its16 reviewed source items are not a known proof-leaf total (null). Chapter2 remains incomplete; Chapters3–16 remain unenumerated, with necessary appendices required. The whole Goal is active.'
cost='The state is a mathematical noncomputable exact-real pair (count,value). The count grows; there is no fixed-bit memory, executable implementation or runtime certificate. Strict-prefix equality compares the same fixed c and gives no causality guarantee if an external caller chooses c from future data.'
fixtures='Six public canaries check distinct initial states and their identical first update; varying means1/2 and2/3; unchanged prediction before a changed current target and changed next state; feasible initial1 and outside-initial2 at time0; the half-state two-round regret3/4 with the prior bound; and feasible initial1,target0 giving first loss1>1/4.'
formula=r'\bar y_0=0,\quad\bar y_t=\frac1t\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}y_i\ (t>0);\quad p_c(t)=\begin{cases}c&t=0\\\bar y_t&t>0\end{cases};\quad F((n,m),z)=\left(n+1,m+\frac{z-m}{n+1}\right);\quad S_c(0)=(0,c),\ S_c(t+1)=F(S_c(t),y_t);\quad S_c(t)=(t,p_c(t))'
explain='The all-time mean successor identity has positive real denominator t+1. At t=0 the empty empirical mean is0 but the actual prediction is c. The first recursive update cancels c and gives (1,y0). For t>0 induction and the proved mean successor identity give the complete state identity. Existing decomposition, minimization and uniqueness prove the positive-prefix mean is the actual hindsight squared-loss leader; interval feasibility makes it an admissible comparator. The prediction uses the previous prefix, rather than an argmin or state oracle.'
